<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Exercise for the Lagrange Multipliers chapter, collected in the Exercises chapter.
-->
<worksheet xml:id="exercises-lagrange">
  <title> Exercises for <xref ref="section-lagrange-multipliers" text="title"/></title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      If you would like to review this topic before attempting the exercises,
      click <xref ref="section-lagrange-multipliers" text="title"/>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-parallel-tangent-plane">
      <title>Tangent Planes Parallel to a Given Plane</title>

      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the points on the surface
          <me>x^2 + y^2 + 3z^2 = 1</me>
          at which the tangent plane is parallel to the plane
          <m>-x - 5y - 2z = -3</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <solution>
        <p>
          Multiplying by <m>-1</m>, the given plane is
          <m>x + 5y + 2z = 3</m>, with normal vector
          <m>\mathbf{n} = \langle 1, 5, 2\rangle</m>.
          The tangent plane to the ellipsoid is parallel to this plane exactly
          at the points of the ellipsoid that are closest to and farthest from
          it, so we minimize and maximize the distance to the plane subject to
          the constraint of lying on the ellipsoid.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig-lagrange-schematic">
          <caption>
            A schematic cross-section.  The tangent plane to the ellipsoid is
            parallel to the given plane exactly at the closest point <m>S_1</m>
            and the farthest point <m>S_2</m>, where <m>\nabla g</m> is
            parallel to the plane's normal <m>\mathbf{n}</m>.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="img-lagrange-schematic" width="75%">
            <prefigure label="lagrange-schematic" xmlns="https://prefigure.org">
              <diagram dimensions="(500,330)" margins="5">
                <definition>S1 = (sqrt(2), sqrt(2)/2)</definition>
                <definition>S2 = (-sqrt(2), -sqrt(2)/2)</definition>
                <definition>P = (2.5, 1)</definition>
                <coordinates bbox="[-3.6,-2.3,5.6,3.3]">

                  <group at="constraint">
                    <ellipse at="ellipse" center="(0,0)" axes="(2,1)"
                             stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
                    <label at="constraint-label" anchor="(0.4,-1.12)" alignment="southeast">
                      <m>g(x,y,z)=0</m>
                    </label>
                  </group>

                  <group at="plane">
                    <line at="plane-line" p1="(4.9,-0.2)" p2="(-0.1,2.3)"
                          stroke="black" thickness="3"/>
                    <label at="plane-label" anchor="(0.8,2.35)" alignment="north">
                      <m>x+5y+2z=3</m>
                    </label>
                  </group>

                  <group at="tangencies">
                    <group at="tangency-S1">
                      <line at="tangent-S1" p1="S1" p2="S1+(2,-1)" infinite="yes"
                            stroke="gray" dash="9 5"/>
                      <vector at="gradg-S1" v="(0.65,1.3)" tail="S1"
                              stroke="orange" thickness="3"/>
                      <label at="gradg-S1-label" anchor="S1+(0.65,1.3)" alignment="west">
                        <m>\nabla g</m>
                      </label>
                      <point at="S1-point" p="S1" fill="red" alignment="southwest">
                        <m>S_1</m>
                      </point>
                    </group>
                    <group at="tangency-S2">
                      <line at="tangent-S2" p1="S2" p2="S2+(2,-1)" infinite="yes"
                            stroke="gray" dash="9 5"/>
                      <vector at="gradg-S2" v="(-0.65,-1.3)" tail="S2"
                              stroke="orange" thickness="3"/>
                      <label at="gradg-S2-label" anchor="S2+(-0.65,-1.3)" alignment="southeast">
                        <m>\nabla g</m>
                      </label>
                      <point at="S2-point" p="S2" fill="red" alignment="northeast">
                        <m>S_2</m>
                      </point>
                    </group>
                  </group>

                  <group at="distance">
                    <line at="distance-segment" p1="S1" p2="P"
                          stroke="red" dash="4 4" thickness="2"/>
                  </group>

                  <group at="normal">
                    <vector at="n-vector" v="(0.55,1.1)" tail="P"
                            stroke="blue" thickness="3"/>
                    <label at="n-label" anchor="P+(0.55,1.1)" alignment="northeast">
                      <m>\mathbf{n}=\langle 1,5,2\rangle</m>
                    </label>
                    <point at="P-point" p="P" fill="black" alignment="east">
                      <m>P(3,0,0)</m>
                    </point>
                  </group>

                </coordinates>

                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="figure"
                              text="A schematic cross-section of the ellipsoid together with the given plane, the two tangency points, and the gradient vectors">
                    <annotation ref="constraint"
                                text="The constraint curve g equals 0, a cross-section of the ellipsoid x squared plus y squared plus 3 z squared equals 1"/>
                    <annotation ref="plane"
                                text="The given plane x plus 5 y plus 2 z equals 3, which does not meet the ellipsoid"/>
                    <annotation ref="tangencies"
                                text="The two points on the ellipsoid where the tangent plane is parallel to the given plane">
                      <annotation ref="tangency-S1"
                                  text="At S 1, the point of the ellipsoid closest to the plane, the tangent plane is parallel to the given plane and the gradient of g is parallel to n"/>
                      <annotation ref="tangency-S2"
                                  text="At S 2, the point of the ellipsoid farthest from the plane, the tangent plane is again parallel to the given plane and the gradient of g points opposite to n"/>
                    </annotation>
                    <annotation ref="distance"
                                text="The distance from S 1 to the plane, the quantity minimized in the Lagrange multiplier method"/>
                    <annotation ref="normal"
                                text="The point P equals 3 comma 0 comma 0 chosen on the plane, with the normal vector n equals 1 comma 5 comma 2"/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>

              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          Choose a point on the plane, say <m>P(3,0,0)</m>.
          For a point <m>S(x,y,z)</m> on the ellipsoid, the distance from
          <m>S</m> to the plane is
          <me>
            \frac{\overrightarrow{PS}\cdot\mathbf{n}}{\lvert\mathbf{n}\rvert}
            = \frac{\langle x-3,\, y,\, z\rangle\cdot\langle 1,5,2\rangle}
                   {\lvert\mathbf{n}\rvert}.
          </me>
          Since <m>\lvert\mathbf{n}\rvert</m> is constant, it suffices to
          optimize the numerator
          <me>
            f(x,y,z) = x - 3 + 5y + 2z
          </me>
          subject to the constraint
          <me>
            g(x,y,z) = x^2 + y^2 + 3z^2 - 1 = 0.
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          The Lagrange condition <m>\nabla f = \lambda\, \nabla g</m> gives
          <me>
            \langle 1, 5, 2\rangle = \lambda \langle 2x,\, 2y,\, 6z\rangle,
          </me>
          so
          <me>
            x = \frac{1}{2\lambda}, \qquad
            y = \frac{5}{2\lambda}, \qquad
            z = \frac{1}{3\lambda}.
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          Substituting into the constraint <m>x^2 + y^2 + 3z^2 = 1</m>,
          <md>
            <mrow>\frac{1}{4\lambda^2} + \frac{25}{4\lambda^2} + \frac{3}{9\lambda^2} \amp= 1</mrow>
            <mrow>\frac{3 + 75 + 4}{12\lambda^2} \amp= 1</mrow>
            <mrow>\frac{1}{\lambda^2} \amp= \frac{12}{82},</mrow>
          </md>
          so that
          <me>
            \frac{1}{\lambda} = \pm 2\sqrt{\frac{3}{82}}.
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>
          Therefore
          <me>
            x = \pm\sqrt{\frac{3}{82}}, \qquad
            y = \pm 5\sqrt{\frac{3}{82}}, \qquad
            z = \pm\sqrt{\frac{2}{123}},
          </me>
          and the two points are
          <me>
            S_1\!\left(\sqrt{\tfrac{3}{82}},\; 5\sqrt{\tfrac{3}{82}},\; \sqrt{\tfrac{2}{123}}\right)
            \qquad\text{and}\qquad
            S_2\!\left(-\sqrt{\tfrac{3}{82}},\; -5\sqrt{\tfrac{3}{82}},\; -\sqrt{\tfrac{2}{123}}\right).
          </me>
          The positive signs give <m>S_1</m>, the point closest to the plane,
          and the negative signs give <m>S_2</m>, the farthest.  At both
          points the tangent plane is parallel to the plane
          <m>x + 5y + 2z = 3</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
</worksheet>
